Refer to the Tension Members section in the Civil Engineering chapter of the FE Reference Handbook (v10.6).

Ultimately, we must solve for the design tensile strength. The handbook requires checking yielding and rupture:

$$\begin{gathered}
\text{Yielding: } \phi_y P_n = 0.90 F_y A_g \\
\text{Rupture: } \phi_f P_n = 0.75 F_u A_e \\
A_e = U A_n \\
U = 1 - \frac{x}{L}
\end{gathered}$$

List the known and unknown parameters:

$$\begin{aligned}
U &= 1 - \frac{0.90}{4.50} = 0.80 \\
A_e &= (0.80)(3.00) = 2.40\ \text{in}^2 \\
\phi_y P_n &= 0.90(36)(3.50) = 113.4\ \text{kip} \\
\phi_f P_n &= 0.75(58)(2.40) = 104.4\ \text{kip}
\end{aligned}$$

The smaller value governs:

$$\begin{aligned}
\text{design strength} &= \min(113.4,\ 104.4) \\
&= 104.4\ \text{kip}
\end{aligned}$$

Source: EPA SWMM Reference Manual Volume I, Hydrology (EPA/600/R-15/162A), Chapter 4, Horton's method, Equations 4-1 to 4-3 (https://downloads.tuflow.com/SWMM/SWMM5_Reference_Manual_Volume1_Hydrology_P100NYRA.pdf). Horton's equation is not in the FE Reference Handbook, so it is given in the question. This is supplemental practice.

Ultimately, we must find the actual infiltration rate, which is the lesser of the capacity and the rainfall intensity.

First the capacity at \(t = 0.5\) hr:

$$\begin{aligned}
f_p &= 0.5 + (4.0 - 0.5)e^{-(3)(0.5)} \\
&= 1.28\ \text{in/hr}
\end{aligned}$$

Then compare with the rainfall intensity:

$$\begin{aligned}
f &= \min(f_p,\ i) \\
&= \min(1.28,\ 1.0) \\
&= 1.00\ \text{in/hr}
\end{aligned}$$
